GUM_court_mountain	739-744	745-771	How is that not commercial ?	I mean , we found in Kleinhans -- Kleinhans that short term rentals are commercial use that violate a covenant , that says no commercial use .	How is that not commercial?	I mean, we found in Kleinhans -- Kleinhans that short term rentals are commercial use that violate a covenant, that says no commercial use.	739-744	745-771	How is that not commercial ?	I mean , we found in Kleinhans -- Kleinhans that short term rentals are commercial use that violate a covenant , that says no commercial use .	1<2	implicit	expansion.conjunction	conjunction
GUM_court_mountain	739-803	804-1292	How is that not commercial ? I mean , we found in Kleinhans -- Kleinhans that short term rentals are commercial use that violate a covenant , that says no commercial use . So I know that we have the rental covenant . Then we have the no commercial use covenant . How do you weave them together to give both of them meaning ?	Right . Well , so the definitions that the court looked at and the set of covenants in Meyers v Kleinhans were different than they are in this case . In this case , we have a specific set of provisions , one of which is to expressly allow rentals without any reference to duration . And so if you 're going to read the covenant and the contract as a whole , which is required under standard principles of contract instruction -- contract construction , you have to do that in a way that makes those provisions harmonize . And in order to do that , you need to look at what the drafter intended by the definition of commercial . And so the definitions that this court looked to in the Meyers v Kleinhans case would also prohibit long term rentals in this case . And we know that that 's not going to be possible because there is a provision that contemplates rentals . So the type of commercial activities are further defined by the drafter 's intent . You can see in the provision regarding commercial use where it talks about ' such as ' it says , that commercial uses are prohibited , it says " such as junkyards and mobile home parks " . But it also says " etc. " Right ? Correct . And also it says " whatsoever " . So I mean no commercial use whatsoever . It seems very broad to me . I mean , to get the intention . The appellants do n't disagree that the commercial use prohibition is somewhat broad , but it ca n't be read to prohibit rentals . The plaintiffs and the defendants or appellants and appellees both agree that long term rentals are permitted um under the covenants , and there 's no difference between a rental of 28 days and a rental of 31 days . The court in the district um the district court pulled a 30 day limitation out , and it created that limitation onto the contract . There is nothing in the contract that talks about that . Um , and again , the rental use that is being done by the guests or the renters that the Palmers have at their property , um , was the same as it would be no matter what the duration was . Let me follow up on that , because I think they they make the point that that 's regulatory based on the county 's permit . And I 'll , we 'll address that later . But I want to go back to what Justice Gustafson was talking about . So the prohibition is any other commercial ? It does n't say use . It says purpose , " prohibits any other commercial purpose whatsoever . " So whatsoever is a pretty broad term .	How is that not commercial? I mean, we found in Kleinhans -- Kleinhans that short term rentals are commercial use that violate a covenant, that says no commercial use. So I know that we have the rental covenant. Then we have the no commercial use covenant. How do you weave them together to give both of them meaning?	Right. Well, so the definitions that the court looked at and the set of covenants in Meyers v Kleinhans were different than they are in this case. In this case, we have a specific set of provisions, one of which is to expressly allow rentals without any reference to duration. And so if you're going to read the covenant and the contract as a whole, which is required under standard principles of contract instruction -- contract construction, you have to do that in a way that makes those provisions harmonize. And in order to do that, you need to look at what the drafter intended by the definition of commercial. And so the definitions that this court looked to in the Meyers v Kleinhans case would also prohibit long term rentals in this case. And we know that that's not going to be possible because there is a provision that contemplates rentals. So the type